# Copy to .env and fill in. This file is never committed.

# --- Database (PostgreSQL, local) ---
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Web interface ---
# The one password that protects the interface, even for a single user.
DINDON_PASSWORD=change-me-too
DINDON_PORT=8000

# --- Local AI (Ollama) ---
# On a Mac, run Ollama natively (it can use the GPU, Docker cannot): see README.md.
# On a Linux server, use the "ollama" profile of docker-compose.yml and set http://ollama:11434.
OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# Optional Ollama helpers reached through a private network (Tailscale), comma-separated.
# Install the exact same embedding and naming models on each helper. See docs/ordinateurs-analyse.md.
# DINDON_ANALYSIS_WORKERS=http://100.101.102.103:11434,http://100.101.102.104:11434
# The two models of the analysis (see docs/fonctionnement.md). `ollama pull bge-m3` and `ollama pull qwen3:14b` install them.
# bge-m3 gives the vectors (1024 numbers: the database is made for it). The naming model gives a name to each topic that is found;
# `gemma4:12b` is the other one that was compared (more concise, more generic, slower): tools/bench_models.py compares them again.
# DINDON_EMBED_MODEL=bge-m3
# DINDON_NAMING_MODEL=qwen3:14b

# --- Discord: collection (leave empty to only read the exports that you drop in inbox/) ---
# A bot token is recommended. Automating a personal account is against Discord's terms of service
# and can get it closed: see the warning in README.md.
# DISCORD_TOKEN=
# The Activity (the map inside a voice channel, docs/fonctionnement.md). Developer Portal > your application: Application ID (General Information) and
# Client Secret (OAuth2 > Reset Secret). The secret is a password: never commit it.
# DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
# DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=
# One or more server IDs, separated by commas
# DINDON_GUILD_IDS=            empty (or: all) = Dindon follows EVERY server that the bot is in: being invited is the decision.
#                              Or list the ids, separated by commas, on ONE line, to follow only those. (Bot token only.)
# DINDON_GUILD_IDS=
# Seconds between two looks at what is new (30 to 60 is polite; the delay before something shows is about half of it)
# DINDON_POLL_SECONDS=30
# Threads exported with their channel when an account is used: none, active or all
# DINDON_THREADS=active

# --- The rights of the people recorded (docs/regles-du-bot.md) ---
# Messages older than this many days are deleted every day (0 = no limit)
# DINDON_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# true: when the bot is removed from a server (kicked, banned, left, server deleted), everything held of that server is deleted at once
# (messages, members, links, scores, files). Cannot be undone. Empty or false: the data stays (`dindon forget-server <id>` deletes it by hand)
# DINDON_ERASE_ON_REMOVAL=false

# --- Discord: the live bot (mode C) ---
# Uses the same DISCORD_TOKEN and DINDON_GUILD_IDS, which must then be the token of a BOT and the server(s) to follow.
# In the Developer Portal: Bot > Privileged Gateway Intents > switch on "Message Content Intent"; invite the bot to the server
# with the permissions that the "Inviter le bot" window of the interface gives (View Channels, Read Message History, and for the debates: Create Public Threads,
# Send Messages, Send Messages in Threads, Embed Links). Start it with: docker compose --profile bot up -d --build
# To try the bot alone, switch the watcher off (otherwise both bring the same messages, which is harmless but hides the bot):
# DINDON_COLLECTOR=on        (default) the watcher polls Discord and exports what moved, and catches up once a night
# DINDON_COLLECTOR=catchup   only the nightly catch-up: the right companion of the live bot (it fills what a Gateway reconnection missed
#                            and shows edits and deletions), without polling Discord all day
# DINDON_COLLECTOR=off       nothing from the watcher (only the live bot and the exports dropped in inbox/)
# NOTE: DINDON_GUILD_IDS must stay on ONE line (ids separated by commas): a second line silently wins in Docker and loses elsewhere.

# --- The debates (/dindon debat, docs/regles-du-bot.md) ---
# The claims of a debate can be checked on the Internet. THIS SENDS, to a search service, a short neutral sentence about the claim (never the message, never a name) and reads at most
# three of the pages found; nothing else leaves the machine. The members are told by /dindon info and in each debate thread. It is OFF unless you switch it on:
# DINDON_DEBATE_CHECKS=off         off (default): nothing is read, nothing is sent.   observe: the claims are checked and NOTED in the database; nothing is published.
#                                  Read the result with: docker compose exec bot dindon debate-report
#                                  answer: Dindon ANSWERS FIRST, with its local model, without the Internet, when it is certain that a claim is false: the answer is public, has no source and is
#                                  judged by the participants (Valide / Invalide). Only if there is more Invalide does it look on the Internet and correct itself. Works without a search service.
#                                  live: answer, AND the corrections that trusted sources make by themselves. It only works if the next line is given.
# DINDON_DEBATE_PRECISION=         the precision of « contredit » that `python tools/measure_claims.py verify` measured on YOUR set (docs/regles-du-bot.md « Mesure »). Without it, or under
#                                  the threshold (0.90 by default, DINDON_DEBATE_MIN_PRECISION), `live` stays at `answer`: the bot says so in its log and in `dindon preflight`.
# DINDON_DEBATE_MODEL=qwen3:14b    the local model that reads the messages (it must be installed: `ollama pull qwen3:14b`)
# At least one search service is needed:
# DINDON_FACTCHECK_API_KEY=        the key of Google's Fact Check Tools API (fact-checks already published by press desks). A secret: put it here yourself.
# DINDON_SEARXNG_URL=              the address of a SearXNG that you run: http://searxng:8080 with `docker compose --profile search up -d searxng` (and SEARXNG_SECRET below)
# SEARXNG_SECRET=                  any long random text, for the SearXNG of the "search" profile
